<article class="env-proposition" id="02WN">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02WN">Proposition <span data-tag="02WN">7.1.5.10</span>.</a> Let $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor of $\infty $-categories and let $f: X \rightarrow Y$ be a morphism in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ with the property that $U(f)$ is an isomorphism. Then any two of the following three conditions imply the third: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The object $X$ is $U$-initial. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The object $Y$ is $U$-initial. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(3)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The morphism $f$ is an isomorphism. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Fix an object $Z \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. We claim that any two of the following three conditions imply the third: 
    <p>
<dl>
<dt>$(1_{Z})$</dt>
<dd><p>
The functor $U$ induces a homotopy equivalence $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( X,Z) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}( U(X), U(Z) )$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2_{Z})$</dt>
<dd><p>
The functor $U$ induces a homotopy equivalence $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( Y,Z) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}( U(Y), U(Z) )$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(3_{Z})$</dt>
<dd><p>
Precomposition $[f]$ induces a homotopy equivalence $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(Y,Z) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}(X,Z)$ (see Notation <a data-tag="01TP" href="/tag/01TP">4.6.8.15</a>). 
</p>
</dd>
</dl>
<p>
       This follows from the commutativity of the diagram 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(Y,Z) \ar [r]^-{ \circ [f] } \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Z) \ar [d] \\ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}( U(Y), U(Z) ) \ar [r]^-{ \circ [ U(f) ] } &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}( U(X), U(Z) ) } \]
</div>
<p>
       in the homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{Kan}}$, since the bottom horizontal map is a homotopy equivalence (by virtue of our assumption that $U(f)$ is an isomorphism). Proposition <a data-tag="02WN" href="/tag/02WN">7.1.5.10</a> follows by allowing the object $Z$ to vary. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></p></p></article>
